Google has changed several things in its smart home system this month: how some people can open the front door, what camera alerts describe, and how the Gemini voice assistant behaves. A company post of 17 November 2026 sets out the headline items, and release notes dated 11 and 25 November add detail. Here is what they say, and the conditions attached, as read on 27 November 2026.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Google competes with Anthropic. Stackbrook did not test any of these features or devices and has no affiliate relationship with Google at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated. This is general information, not financial or legal advice.*

## The changes at a glance

| Change | Who it is for | Status Google gives |
|---|---|---|
| Digital home keys in Google Wallet | Android users with a compatible lock | Public preview |
| Audio detection in camera event descriptions | Home Premium Advanced | Announced 17 November |
| Estimated thermostat savings | Owners in the US and Canada | Starting in December |
| Faster everyday Gemini requests | Gemini for Home users | Google says up to 20% faster |
| Improved Gemini Live | Early access homes with Home Premium and a compatible speaker or display | Rolling out over the coming weeks |
| Six more countries for Gemini for Home | See the list below | Listed as coming in December |

## Home keys: unlocking the door with a phone

Google says some front door locks can now be opened from an Android phone, either by tapping or automatically as you walk up, using a digital home key stored in Google Wallet. The 11 November release notes label this a public preview.

The preview help page spells out the hardware: a compatible lock and an Android phone with Aliro NFC or Ultra Wideband. NFC covers tap to unlock, and Ultra Wideband covers hands-free unlocking. The release notes describe the locks as Aliro-compliant. According to the same page, this is for locks that belong to you and whose settings you control. An added key syncs to your compatible devices that have Wallet under the same Gmail account. We found no minimum Android version or list of supported lock models on the pages read.

To join, Google's instructions are:

1. In your Google Groups settings, check that **Add me to their groups** is ticked, then save.
2. In the Google Home app, tap your profile picture or initial, then **Public Preview**.
3. Choose **Join Public Preview**.
4. To add the key, tap your profile picture or initial, then **Settings**, then **Google Wallet key**. The help page also gives a route through the lock's device features.

This is preview software controlling a door, so keep another way in, and think about who can use any compatible device signed in to that account.

## Camera alerts and controls

Google Home Premium Advanced can now pick up sounds such as a barking dog or breaking glass in the descriptions of motion-triggered camera and doorbell events, according to the 17 November post. The Premium pages also describe sound detection by speakers and displays, covering breaking glass or smoke and carbon monoxide alarms, and the help page says sound detection is limited to the US. The 17 November post gives no country list for the new camera descriptions, and we could not tell whether the US limit applies to them.

The 25 November notes add:

- A control to start or stop a 30-second siren while you watch live video, on the Nest Cam Outdoor (wired, 2nd gen), the onn Outdoor Camera Plug-In and the onn Floodlight Camera Hardwired.
- Familiar face alerts on the two onn cameras, which need a Home Premium subscription. For Nest cameras and doorbells, the help page says these alerts are unavailable in certain places, giving Illinois in the US as an example, and the feature may not work in every location or on every camera. We found no region list specific to the onn models.
- Simpler one-step setup of camera features for new cameras for Premium users in the US. Google says existing settings do not change.

If your cameras face visitors, staff or neighbours, check the rules where you live before switching on face recognition or audio features.

## Thermostat changes

Google says that from December its app will show Nest thermostat owners in the US and Canada a running estimate of what they have saved. The post also says the 4th-gen Nest Learning Thermostat can work with compatible room sensors from other makers to reduce uneven temperatures. The 25 November notes add the option to copy one day's schedule to other days on that model and on the Nest Thermostat. Treat the savings figure as an estimate; Google's post gives no method for it.

## Gemini for Home: speed, voice and new countries

Google says Gemini for Home now handles everyday requests up to 20% faster. That is the company's figure, and we saw no test conditions. The 11 November notes say that when several devices match a request, the early access assistant now asks a clarifying question instead of guessing.

The 25 November notes say an improved Gemini Live, started by saying "Hey Google, let's chat", is rolling out over the coming weeks and can handle daily actions such as timers and lights. The Gemini help page adds that Gemini Live needs Home Premium and a compatible device, and that older hardware such as the original Google Home and Home Max does not get it.

Separately, the same notes say story requests in early access started getting more expressive voices on 25 November, and you can steer a story while it is being told.

Google's post says Gemini for Home is expanding to six more countries, including India and Brazil. The help page lists six as coming in December: Brazil, Colombia, India, Singapore, South Korea and Taiwan. It lists Portuguese, Korean and Traditional Chinese as coming in December too. The page already names 20 early access countries, among them the US, the UK, Canada, Australia, Germany, France, Japan and Mexico.

### Read this before you switch

According to the help page, once a home moves to Gemini for Home it **cannot go back** to Google Assistant, and the change applies to everyone in that home. Early access is also limited to selected languages and countries. The troubleshooting section calls for a personal Google account instead of a Workspace one, a supported assistant language, Google Home app 4.1 or later, and recent enough speaker or display software.

## What needs a subscription

Basic voice tasks such as controlling devices, playing media, setting timers and asking general questions are on the free tier. The paid extras sit in Google Home Premium.

![Table diagram of Google Home Premium plans: Standard at 10 dollars a month with Gemini Live and 30 days of event video history, Advanced at 20 dollars with 60 days, 10 days of continuous history, Home Brief and descriptive notifications](/images/site1/google-home-november-2026-update-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages. US store prices as read on 27 November 2026.*

On the US Google Store, Standard costs $10 a month or $100 a year, and Advanced costs $20 a month or $200 a year, as of 27 November 2026. Standard includes 30 days of event-based video history, familiar face alerts (not everywhere), package alerts (the help page ties these to Nest doorbells) and Gemini Live on compatible devices. Features vary by device and country, and Premium needs a supported country. Advanced raises event history to 60 days, adds 10 days of round-the-clock history for cameras and wired doorbells, and includes descriptive notifications, video history search and daily summaries. The help page limits round-the-clock history to Nest cameras and doorbells on wired power, and says the battery Nest Doorbell does not support it. Google's help pages also place Home Brief, a daily evening summary of notable camera events, in Advanced.

The help page says Premium can also be activated through a Google AI Pro plan or higher, but the pages we read did not say which Premium tier that gives. Check this in your own account before paying twice. Prices and availability outside the US may differ.

## What to do this week

- Open the Google Home app and check which plan you have, and whether a Google AI plan already includes Premium.
- Before accepting the Gemini switch, ask the others in your home, since there is no way back.
- If you want home keys, confirm that both your lock and your phone meet the requirements before joining the preview.
- Review which cameras have face or sound features turned on.

Google's release notes carry their own caution that they are generated with the help of Gemini and may need corrections, so check the app for what your home actually has.
